# Q: does a defensive structure shoot on its own, at what, and at what does it refuse to?
#
# Run against the emplacement fixture, which clears the map and puts three enemy machines on the
# clearest ground it can find — a tank and an aircraft 72 m out, and a tank 150 m out — and a
# command centre behind them to raise the buildings from:
#
#   MiVic.Game.exe --emplacement-demo --probe tools/probe/emplacement.probe `
#                  --probe-out artifacts/probe/emplacement.txt
#
# The point of the fixture is that nothing in this script gives an order to shoot. The two
# emplacements are placed through `structure ... build`, which is the command a player's click on
# the build panel issues, and after that the script only advances the clock and asks what happened.
# A defensive structure that had to be told to fire would never fire in a real game, because the
# player has no reason to think of it — so "it fired with nobody having told it to" is the whole
# feature, and the `events` stream is where it is visible.
#
# The two enemies are placed to make the refusals visible as well as the shots. The gun cannot
# touch an aircraft and the anti-aircraft emplacement cannot touch a tank, and in both cases the
# machine it cannot touch is *shooting it* at the time: an enemy that is in range, visible,
# hostile, and simply not a target.

tick 1

# The line-up, so the slots and the distances below are the fixture's own numbers rather than
# anything this script assumes.
units western 10

# --- the gun emplacement -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The site is the middle of the clearing. The order is the player's own path: the site is
# surveyed, the cost is paid, and what appears is a building site that does nothing until it is
# up — which the `structures` line below shows, and which is why the emplacement is already
# damaged before it has fired a shot.
structure GunEmplacement 23 -192 0 build
tick 1
structures 0

# Eight seconds of construction, and then it is a gun.
tick 180
structures 0

# The first shot, two ticks after it finished rising: no order was given to it between the
# `structure` line above and this one, and this is the event that says so. The window is wide
# because the two enemies that can reach it are firing at it every few ticks as well, and the
# line worth reading is the one that names slot 505 as the shooter rather than as the target.
tick 2
events 16

# And three seconds of it being a gun. The events are the proof: shot events from slot 505, hit
# events on the tank it chose, and no event at all against slot 507 — the aircraft, which is
# nearer to it than the far tank is and which is firing at it throughout.
tick 58
events 8
unit 505
units western 10

# --- the anti-aircraft emplacement ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# Raised thirty metres behind the gun, which is far enough that the two footprints do not share a
# cell. It is the same order through the same command; what differs is what it will shoot at.
structure AntiAirEmplacement 23 -160 0 build
tick 1
tick 200
structures 0

# And three seconds of the pair of them firing at the two different halves of the enemy line: the
# gun at the ground, the anti-aircraft emplacement at the aircraft, and neither at the other's.
tick 60
events 8
units western 10

# Look at it: a gun pit and an anti-aircraft mount, thirty metres apart, both of them turned
# towards what they are shooting at, with the enemy line to the east of them.
focus 30 -178
zoom 95
pitch -0.52
yaw 0.62
shot artifacts/probe/emplacements.png
